Lua script test configuration (lua/)
====================================

The 'lua' test shows how a Lua script supplies values to the filter.  The script
registers a sample fetch, a converter and an action, and the OTel scopes read
what they produce: the fetch maps the first segment of the request path to an
area name, the converter masks the digits of the last segment, and the action
stores the request method in a transaction variable.

The configuration is the runnable form of the example in section 5.1 of the
configuration guide.  It needs an HAProxy build with Lua support: without the
USE_LUA=1 option, haproxy refuses the Lua keywords of lua/haproxy.cfg.


Files
-----

  lua/otel.cfg      OTel filter configuration (instrumentation and scopes)
  lua/haproxy.cfg   HAProxy frontend/backend configuration
  lua/otel.yml      Exporter, processor, reader and provider definitions
  lua/otel-lua.lua  The Lua script: one fetch, one converter, one action
  run-lua.sh        Convenience script to launch HAProxy with this config


Lua values
----------

The script runs on the HAProxy thread that handles the stream, so the filter
evaluates what it returns like any other sample.  The configuration name each
registration takes, and where the scenario reads it:

  Registration                          Name           Read by
  -------------------------------------------------------------------
  core.register_fetches("otel_area")    lua.otel_area  attribute lua.area
  core.register_converters("otel_tail") lua.otel_tail  attribute lua.tail
  core.register_action("otel_tag")      lua.otel_tag   http-request rule

The action writes 'txn.otel_method' and the 'switching_rules_request' scope
reads it back through 'var(txn.otel_method)'.


Events
------

  T = Trace (span)    L = Log (log-record)

  Stream lifecycle events:

    Event                          Scope                     T  L
    ------------------------------------------------------------------
    on-stream-start                on_stream_start           x  -

  Request analyzer events:

    Event                          Scope                     T  L
    ------------------------------------------------------------------
    on-frontend-http-request       frontend_http_request     x  x
    on-switching-rules-request     switching_rules_request   x  -

The order matters here.  The 'on-frontend-http-request' event fires before the
http-request rules, where the Lua action runs, so a scope bound to that event
cannot see 'txn.otel_method'.  The variable is read by a scope bound only to
'on-switching-rules-request', the first event after those rules.  A fetch or a
converter needs no such care: the filter evaluates it in the run of the scope
that names it.


What to look for
----------------

A request to /api/v1/users/42 leaves:

  "HTTP request"            attribute lua.area "service"
                            attribute lua.tail "/api/v1/users/N"
  "Switching rules request" attribute lua.method "GET"
  log record "lua-request"  attribute lua.area "service"
                            body "service /api/v1/users/N"

The log body takes its last part from the converter, so the request path appears
there with its digits masked.

The fetch returns 'service' only when the complete first path segment is 'api'
or 'graphql'; names such as '/api2' and '/api-v2' return 'site'.  Requests that
have no path, such as 'OPTIONS *', also return 'site' without a Lua error.  The
'lua.tail' attribute is empty, and the path-based log record is not emitted.


Running the test
----------------

The run-lua.sh script (a symlink to run-test-config.sh) runs this configuration.
It invokes haproxy with -f haproxy-common.cfg -f lua/haproxy.cfg.  The origin
server must be running on 127.0.0.1:8000.  From the test/ directory:

  % ./run-lua.sh [/path/to/haproxy] [pidfile] [logfile]

Or run it manually:

  % /path/to/haproxy -f haproxy-common.cfg -f lua/haproxy.cfg
